\name{ScreenBuffer}
\alias{ScreenBuffer}
\title{Virtual screen (framebuffer)}
\description{
A \code{ScreenBuffer} is an in-memory grid of terminal cells. Widgets are
painted into a buffer, buffers are compared with \code{\link[=diff_screen]{diff_screen()}} and only
the differences are sent to the terminal.

Cells are stored in four \verb{height x width} matrices (\code{chars}, \code{fg}, \code{bg},
\code{attrs}) indexed as \verb{[row, column]}. A wide character occupies two
columns: the first holds the character, the second holds \code{""} (a
continuation cell). All writes keep this invariant, so a wide character
is never left half-overwritten.

Use \code{\link[=screen_buffer]{screen_buffer()}} to create one.
}
